    <statement><p>Calculate the length of the path over the given interval.
<me>(\sin 4 t, \cos 4 t), \, 0 \le t \le \pi</me></p>
<p><fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>4\pi </m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>x = \sin 4 t, \, y = \cos 4 t</m>, hence <m>x' = 4 \cos 4 t</m> and <m>y' = - 4 \sin 4 t</m>.  By the formula for the arc length we obtain:
<me>S = \int ^\pi _0 \sqrt{x'(t)^2 + y'(t)^2} \, dt = \int ^\pi _0 \sqrt{16 \cos^2 4 t + 16 \sin^2 4 t} \, dt = \int ^\pi _0 \sqrt{16} \, dt = 4 \pi</me>


</solution>
